#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# sequence_cases

#% text
# `sequence_cases(xs, p)` gives the runs of consecutive elements of `xs` that match the list pattern `p`, such as `[a, b]` or `[x_, x_]`.

#% text
# `sequence_cases(xs, p -> r)` gives `r` for each run, with the pattern's names bound.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Runs are found from the left and do not overlap; at each start the longest matching run is taken.

#% item
# A list pattern with sequence variables (`[a, __]`) matches runs of several lengths.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
sequence_cases([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3], [x_, x_])
#% output
#= [[2, 2], [3, 3]]

#% cell
sequence_cases([a, b, c, a, b], [a, b])
#% output
#= [[a, b], [a, b]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A rule gives a value per run:

#% cell
sequence_cases([1, 2, 5, 6], [x_, y_] when y == x + 1 -> x + y)
#% output
#= [3, 11]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `sequence_count` counts the same runs:

#% cell
xs = [1, 1, 2, 1, 1]
length(sequence_cases(xs, [1, 1])) == sequence_count(xs, [1, 1])
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `sequence_position`, `sequence_replace`, `sequence_split`, `cases`, `partition`
